Fix \(e=(u,c,c')\in\mathcal E_{\mathrm{per}}\), and write
\[
\sigma_{c,1}=1,\qquad \sigma_{c,0}=-1,\qquad
\sigma_{c',1}=-1,\qquad \sigma_{c',0}=1.
\]
The four pairs indexed by \((a,s)\) below are \((c,1),(c,0),(c',1),(c',0)\).  By the definitions of \(p_{a,s}\) and \(m_{a,s,u}\), positivity of the candidate propensity, and iterated conditioning, for each of those pairs,
\[
\begin{aligned}
&E_P\!\left[
 \frac{\mathbf 1_{a,s}}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}
 \{\Delta Y_u-\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)\}
 \mathrel{\Big|}X\right]\\
&\qquad=
\frac{p_{a,s}(X)}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}
\{m_{a,s,u}(X)-\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)\}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{1}
\]
The candidate restriction level appearing in \(S_e\) is
\(\widetilde g_e(X)=\sum_{(a,s)}\sigma_{a,s}\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)\), whereas
\(g_e(X)=\sum_{(a,s)}\sigma_{a,s}m_{a,s,u}(X)\).  Summing (1) with the four signs and adding \(\widetilde g_e-g_e\) therefore gives the exact identity
\[
\begin{aligned}
E_P[S_e(O;\widetilde\eta)\mid X]-g_e(X)
&=\sum_{(a,s)}\sigma_{a,s}
 \{\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)-m_{a,s,u}(X)\}
 \left\{1-\frac{p_{a,s}(X)}{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}\right\}\\
&=\sum_{(a,s)}\sigma_{a,s}
 \{\widetilde m_{a,s,u}(X)-m_{a,s,u}(X)\}
 \frac{\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)-p_{a,s}(X)}
      {\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{2}
\]
Thus every summand is the product of a propensity error and a regression error.  Along any differentiable one-dimensional path through the truth \(\widetilde\eta=\eta=(p,m)\), both errors vanish at path value zero, so differentiating their product at zero gives zero.  This proves the asserted Gateaux orthogonality in either nuisance coordinate.

The clipping premise and Assumption \(\ref{ass:cell-overlap}\) give
\(1/\widetilde p_{a,s}(X)\le 2/\varepsilon\) almost surely.  Applying the triangle inequality to (2), then H\"older's inequality coordinatewise, yields
\[
\begin{aligned}
\|E_P[S_e(O;\widetilde\eta)\mid X]-g_e(X)\|_{P_X,2}
&\le \frac{2}{\varepsilon}
 \sum_{(a,s)}
 \|\widetilde p_{a,s}-p_{a,s}\|_{P_X,4}
 \|\widetilde m_{a,s,u}-m_{a,s,u}\|_{P_X,4}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{3}
\]
Because \(\mathcal E_{\mathrm{per}}\) and all cell and period index sets are finite, summing (3) over the retained coordinates and using equivalence of finite-dimensional Euclidean norms gives
\[
\|E_P[S^{\mathrm{per}}(O;\widetilde\eta)\mid X]-g(X)\|_{P_X,2}
\le C_\varepsilon
\|\widetilde p-p\|_{P_X,4}
\|\widetilde m-m\|_{P_X,4},
\tag{4}
\]
where \(C_\varepsilon<\infty\) depends only on \(\varepsilon\) and the fixed index sets.

Finally, Definition \(\ref{def:orthogonal-complete-normal-signal}\) gives, at the truth,
\[
S_e(O;\eta)=g_e(X)+Z^{\mathrm{per}}_{e,P}(O).
\tag{5}
\]
Every row \(e\in\mathcal E_{\mathrm{per}}\) is one of the localized observable equality rows.  Hence Theorem \(\ref{thm:observable-equivalence}\), equivalently Definition \(\ref{def:local-observed-model}\), implies \(g_e(X)=0\) \(P\)-almost surely.  Equation (5) then gives
\(S^{\mathrm{per}}(O;\eta)=Z_P^{\mathrm{per}}(O)\) almost surely, as claimed.

Let the support of \(X\) be \(\{x_1,\ldots,x_D\}\), where \(D<\infty\), and let \(\min_dP(X=x_d)\ge\underline p_X>0\).  For fold \(\ell\), define \(n_{d,-\ell}=\sum_{i\notin I_\ell}\mathbf 1\{X_i=x_d\}\).  The numbers of cohorts, eligibility cells, periods, normal coordinates, and folds are fixed.  Assumption \(\ref{ass:iid-fixed-panel}\) and the support-mass condition imply that every training count for a support point and every retained cohort--eligibility cell is of order \(n\) in probability, uniformly over the fixed number of folds.  Assumption \(\ref{ass:cell-overlap}\) also gives
\[
P(X=x_d,G=a,S=s)
=P(X=x_d)p_{a,s}(x_d)
\ge \underline p_X\varepsilon.
\tag{6}
\]
Consequently all empirical denominators used by the cell averages exceed one half of their population denominators with probability tending to one.

We first establish the oracle moment rate.  Each coordinate of \(Z_P^{\mathrm{per}}\) is a sum of four centered outcome increments multiplied by inverse propensities.  Each coordinate of \(\psi_P^0\) is a fixed finite sum of the same type, together with conditional-mean and target-share terms.  Assumptions \(\ref{ass:cell-overlap}\) and \(\ref{ass:conditional-fourth-envelope}\), the fixed value of \(T\), and conditional Jensen inequalities therefore imply, at this fixed law,
\[
\max_{1\le d\le D}
E_P\!\left[
 \|Z_P^{\mathrm{per}}(O)\|^4+|\psi_P^0(O)|^4
 \mathrel{\Big|}X=x_d\right]<\infty.
\tag{7}
\]
In particular, every coordinate of
\(Z_P^{\mathrm{per}}Z_P^{\mathrm{per}\prime}\) and of
\(Z_P^{\mathrm{per}}\psi_P^0\) has finite conditional variance.  For any such coordinate \(U\), independence and (6) give
\[
\operatorname{Var}_P\!\left(
 \frac1{n_{d,-\ell}}
 \sum_{i\notin I_\ell}\mathbf 1\{X_i=x_d\}U_i
 \mathrel{\Big|}n_{d,-\ell}\right)
=\frac{\operatorname{Var}_P(U\mid X=x_d)}{n_{d,-\ell}}.
\tag{8}
\]
Chebyshev's inequality applied to (8), followed by a union bound over the finitely many coordinates, support points, and folds, shows that all oracle conditional second and cross-moment averages differ from their population values by \(O_P(n^{-1/2})\).

It remains to account for nuisance estimation.  Assumption \(\ref{ass:crossfit-independence}\) makes every foldwise object considered here a function only of the corresponding training observations.  In the fixed-support construction stipulated in the proposition, the entries of \(\widehat p^{-\ell}\), \(\widehat m^{-\ell}\), the preliminary target shares and ratios, \(\widehat K^{-\ell}\), and \(\widehat r^{-\ell}\) are finite-dimensional functions of empirical cell probabilities, empirical first moments of outcome increments, and empirical second cross-moments of outcome increments.  The variances of all these raw empirical moments are finite by Assumption \(\ref{ass:conditional-fourth-envelope}\).  Thus the same calculation as (8) makes their joint error \(O_P(n^{-1/2})\).  On the event following (6), every denominator in these finite-dimensional functions is bounded away from zero.  Expanding each reciprocal by
\[
\widehat d^{-1}-d^{-1}
=-\frac{\widehat d-d}{d^2}
+\frac{(\widehat d-d)^2}{d^2\widehat d}
\tag{9}
\]
and expanding the remaining finite products shows directly that substituting the estimated cell moments changes each oracle second or cross moment by \(O_P(n^{-1/2})\).  The product-drift identity (2) in Lemma \(\ref{lem:orthogonal-normal-signal}\) additionally shows that the conditional centering drift of \(\widehat S^{-\ell}\) is a product of two root-\(n\) cell-average errors and hence is \(O_P(n^{-1})\), so no first-order regression-centering term has been omitted.  Because a norm over a fixed finite support is a finite weighted sum and all matrix dimensions are fixed, we obtain, uniformly over folds,
\[
\|\widehat K^{-\ell}-K_P\|_{P_X,4,\mathrm{op}}
+\|\widehat r^{-\ell}-r_P\|_{P_X,4}
=O_P(n^{-1/2}).
\tag{10}
\]

We next prove rank recovery and the inverse rate.  For a support point \(x_d\), let \(r_d=\operatorname{rank}K_P(x_d)\), and let \(\kappa_d\) be its smallest positive eigenvalue, with \(\kappa_d=+\infty\) if \(r_d=0\).  Since there are finitely many support points,
\[
\underline\kappa
=\min_{d:r_d>0}\kappa_d>0,
\tag{11}
\]
with the convention \(\underline\kappa=+\infty\) when every rank is zero.  This is a pointwise consequence of finite support, not a uniform lower-eigenvalue condition on the model class.  For symmetric matrices \(B\) and \(C\), the bound
\(|v'(B-C)v|\le\|B-C\|_{\mathrm{op}}\) for every unit vector \(v\), applied to the variational characterizations of their ordered eigenvalues, gives
\[
\max_j|\lambda_j(B)-\lambda_j(C)|
\le\|B-C\|_{\mathrm{op}}.
\tag{12}
\]
Because \(n^{-1/2}/\tau_n\to0\), (10) implies
\[
\max_{d,\ell}
\|\widehat K^{-\ell}(x_d)-K_P(x_d)\|_{\mathrm{op}}
=o_P(\tau_n).
\tag{13}
\]
Assumption \(\ref{ass:rank-threshold-vanishing}\) and (11) make \(\tau_n<\underline\kappa/2\) eventually whenever a positive eigenvalue exists.  Equations (12)--(13) then show simultaneously that every population positive eigenvalue is retained and every eigenvalue corresponding to the population null space lies below the threshold.  The thresholded estimator therefore recovers \(r_d\) at every support point, uniformly over the fixed folds, with probability tending to one.

For completeness, the inverse perturbation step can be obtained without an additional rank hypothesis.  Conditional on rank recovery, at every support point with \(r_d>0\) the retained spectrum of \(\widehat K^{-\ell}(x_d)\) lies in \([\kappa_d/2,\infty)\).  The positive spectral cluster is separated from zero.  Representing the inverse on that cluster by the finite-dimensional spectral decomposition and using the resolvent identity
\[
(zI-B)^{-1}-(zI-C)^{-1}
=(zI-B)^{-1}(B-C)(zI-C)^{-1}
\tag{14}
\]
on a contour separating zero from the positive spectrum gives a constant \(C_d<\infty\) such that
\[
\|\{\widehat K^{-\ell}_\tau(x_d)\}^{\dagger}
-K_P(x_d)^\dagger\|_{\mathrm{op}}
\le C_d\|\widehat K^{-\ell}(x_d)-K_P(x_d)\|_{\mathrm{op}}.
\tag{15}
\]
When \(r_d=0\), rank recovery makes both pseudoinverses in (15) equal to zero, so the same conclusion holds with any positive \(C_d\).
If \(v\in\ker K_P(x_d)\), then
\(E_P[(v'Z_P^{\mathrm{per}})^2\mid X=x_d]=0\), so
\(v'Z_P^{\mathrm{per}}=0\) conditionally almost surely and therefore
\(v'r_P(x_d)=0\).  Thus \(r_P(x_d)\in\operatorname{range}K_P(x_d)\), including the zero-rank case.  On the recovery event, the triangle inequality gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\|\{\widehat K^{-\ell}_\tau\}^{\dagger}\widehat r^{-\ell}
-K_P^\dagger r_P\|_{P_X,4}\\
&\quad\le
\|\{\widehat K^{-\ell}_\tau\}^{\dagger}\|_{P_X,\infty,\mathrm{op}}
\|\widehat r^{-\ell}-r_P\|_{P_X,4}
+\|\{\widehat K^{-\ell}_\tau\}^{\dagger}-K_P^\dagger\|_{P_X,\infty,\mathrm{op}}
\|r_P\|_{P_X,4}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The first operator norm is bounded by \(2/\underline\kappa\), with the convention \(2/(+\infty)=0\) when all support points have rank zero, while the second is \(O_P(n^{-1/2})\) by (10) and (15).  Taking the maximum over the finite support and folds therefore yields
\[
\|\{\widehat K^{-\ell}_\tau\}^{\dagger}\widehat r^{-\ell}
-K_P^\dagger r_P\|_{P_X,4}
=O_P(n^{-1/2}).
\tag{16}
\]
This statement is unconditional in probability because the rank-recovery event has probability tending to one.  Since \(\tau_n\le1\) eventually, (16) also gives the advertised conservative threshold rate
\[
\|\{\widehat K^{-\ell}_\tau\}^{\dagger}\widehat r^{-\ell}
-K_P^\dagger r_P\|_{P_X,4}
=O_P(n^{-1/2}/\tau_n),
\tag{17}
\]
which is weaker than the pointwise recovered-range rate (16) but does not use its fixed spectral constant in the displayed order.

Finally, if \(\tau_n=n^{-a}\) with \(0<a<1/4\), (17) is
\(O_P(n^{-1/2+a})\), and
\[
\{n^{-1/2+a}\}^2=n^{-1+2a}=o(n^{-1/2}),
\qquad
n^{-1/2+a}n^{-1/2}=n^{-1+a}=o(n^{-1/2}).
\tag{18}
\]
The first relation uses \(a<1/4\), and the second uses the weaker fact \(a<1/2\).  Since the propensity and regression cell-average rates are each \(O_P(n^{-1/2})\), (18) proves the two stated product-rate conclusions.
